{"id": "whdja", "name": "Dja Faunal Reserve", "short": "Dja Faunal Reserve", "place": "Cameroon", "lat": 3.2, "lon": 13.0, "side": "l", "cont": "Africa", "country": "Cameroon", "kind": "Forest", "park": true, "blurb": "Congo Basin rainforest looped by the Dja River: apes, elephants and fruit trees.", "tip": {"id": "fig", "title": "Take away the figs", "text": "Figs feed monkeys, apes, elephants and birds. Set Fig trees to -80% and watch the fruit eaters, then the leopards and eagles that hunt them."}, "sources": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Dja Faunal Reserve", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dja_Faunal_Reserve"}, {"title": "UNESCO World Heritage: Dja Faunal Reserve", "url": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/407"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Western lowland gorilla", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_lowland_gorilla"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: African forest elephant", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_forest_elephant"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: African leopard", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_leopard"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Chimpanzee", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chimpanzee"}], "notes": "The species list comes from GBIF occurrence records inside a circle around the park, so it can include nearby areas. Feeding links come from GloBI records and the sources listed where they state them; others are general ecology and are illustrative.", "imgwiki": "Dja Faunal Reserve", "icon": "elephant", "tier": "overview", "nodes": [{"id": "sun", "sp": "sunlight", "xh": 0.06, "desc": "Light that reaches the forest floor only in gaps and clearings, so canopy trees and understory plants compete hard for it."}, {"id": "rain", "sp": "equatorial rainfall", "xh": 0.2}, {"id": "river", "sp": "dja river and swamps", "xh": 0.38}, {"id": "soil", "sp": "forest soil nutrients", "xh": 0.6, "desc": "Nutrients released from leaf litter and dead wood are taken up quickly by roots and fungi."}, {"id": "decomp", "sp": "fungi and bacteria", "xh": 0.85, "desc": "Fungi, bacteria and soil animals break down fallen leaves, wood and carcasses and return nutrients to the soil."}, {"id": "fig", "sp": "ficus", "xh": 0.1, "desc": "Figs are eaten by monkeys, apes, birds and many other animals, which spread their seeds.", "badge": "Foundation species"}, {"id": "irvingia", "sp": "irvingia gabonensis", "xh": 0.3}, {"id": "diospyros", "sp": "diospyros", "xh": 0.5}, {"id": "marantaceae", "sp": "marantaceae", "xh": 0.7, "desc": "Broad-leaved herbs carpeting forest openings and swampy ground, an important food for gorillas and elephants."}, {"id": "lianas", "sp": "liana", "xh": 0.88}, {"id": "gorilla", "sp": "gorilla gorilla gorilla", "xh": 0.05, "desc": "Mostly a plant eater; it prefers ripe fruit and turns to leaves, herbs and bark when fruit is scarce, and it spreads seeds.", "ic": "quad"}, {"id": "elephant", "sp": "loxodonta cyclotis", "xh": 0.17, "desc": "Eats bark, leaves and many kinds of fruit and carries seeds long distances, which earns it the name megagardener of the forest."}, {"id": "buffalo", "sp": "syncerus caffer nanus", "xh": 0.29}, {"id": "duiker", "sp": "philantomba monticola", "xh": 0.41}, {"id": "spotnose", "sp": "cercopithecus nictitans", "xh": 0.53}, {"id": "mangabey", "sp": "lophocebus albigena", "xh": 0.65}, {"id": "hornbill", "sp": "ceratogymna atrata", "xh": 0.77}, {"id": "termite", "sp": "termitidae", "xh": 0.89, "desc": "Termites eat dead wood and plant litter and are themselves food for apes, reptiles and other insect eaters."}, {"id": "chimp", "sp": "pan troglodytes", "xh": 0.2, "desc": "Eats mostly fruit but also hunts monkeys and small antelopes and fishes for termites with sticks."}, {"id": "chameleon", "sp": "trioceros cristatus", "xh": 0.5}, {"id": "leopard", "sp": "panthera pardus", "xh": 0.3, "desc": "The top cat of the reserve; in Central African forests it preys mainly on duikers and primates, and also takes buffalo and sometimes chimpanzees.", "badge": "Keystone predator"}, {"id": "eagle", "sp": "ste